 6. 🫏 2028 floodgates open 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Illustration: Sarah Grillo/Axios

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Democrats of all political stripes won so big on Tuesday that the party didn't settle its long-running civil war between moderates and progressives, Axios' Holly Otterbein and Alex Thompson write. 

 Instead, the party's historic victories gave each faction some reasons to claim that its brand of Democrats can defeat the GOP in 2028. Why it matters: Democratic operatives are sure of one thing, though: Tuesday's blowout wins mean that more party leaders will be willing to run for the White House than if the midterms hadn't gone so well.

 Barack Obama's 2012 campaign manager, Jim Messina, told Bloomberg this week that he's already met with 29 potential candidates. 🌊 Zoom in: Every wing of the party has a win to point to.

 Progressive Abdul El-Sayed ran on Medicare for All and won Michigan's Senate race by 7 points — a boost for Rep. Alexandria Ocasio-Cortez, who went all-in for him. Faith-forward moderates like James Talarico in Texas and Adam Hamilton in Kansas pulled off huge upsets in deep-red states. Pennsylvania Gov. Josh Shapiro, a centrist technocrat who campaigned on "getting sh*t done," won reelection by 22 points. Keep reading . 

 
 
