 7. 🇮🇷 Trump&#x27;s bad options in Iran 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Photo illustration: Allie Carl/Axios. Photo: Roberto Schmidt/Getty Images

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Post-election, President Trump must decide whether to cut his losses on the Iran war and get a deal — or double down and go back to bombing, Axios' Barak Ravid reports. 

 Why it matters: His options are bad. Going back to war would be highly unpopular, exacerbating the energy crisis while probably not yielding a better military result. But a deal with Iran remains elusive. If the Iranians refuse to make concessions — or, sensing weakness after the president's political loss, even toughen their demands — Trump will have a hard time reaching an agreement.

 One of his former advisers told Axios: "It's one thing to lose the midterms. But Trump doesn't want to look like a loser twice." That's why some Trump advisers think he could ultimately decide to strike Iran again. Keep reading . 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 8. 📊 Polls underestimated the wave 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Illustration: Sarah Grillo/Axios. Stock: Getty Images

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Democrats' midterm tsunami obscured a major polling miss: Surveys correctly anticipated a blue wave, but failed to capture its full force, Axios' Neal Rothschild writes. 

 Why it matters: Pollsters appear to have underestimated Democrats by a wider margin than they underestimated Republicans in 2016 or 2024 — even if they got the headline outcome right this time. 🧮 By the numbers: Democrats are on track to win the national House popular vote by roughly 13 points, compared with a final Silver Bulletin polling average of 8.9 points, according to an initial analysis by The Argument's Lakshya Jain .

 That roughly 4-point gap is similar to the 2020 polling miss that underestimated Republicans. It's larger than the average errors in 2016 and 2024, though comparisons to presidential years are imperfect. Keep reading . 

 
 
